xt":" are the pairs ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"Pair(g, h)"},{"text":". Vertex (g, h) has index ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"index(g)·|V(H)| + index(h)"},{"text":",","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"row-major, the numbering of NamedGraphs’ grids, so ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertex(atIndex:)"},{"text":" costs one division and","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"vertexIndex(of:)"},{"text":" one lookup in each factor.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Rows","type":"text"}],"type":"strong"},{"text":" list the G-moves first, ","type":"text"},{"code":"(g′, h)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" for each entry g′ of G’s row of g, then the H-moves","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"code":"(g, h′)","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":". They are computed from the factors’ index rows and never hash. A factor’s self-loop","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"gives a product self-loop, listed twice in an undirected row as the ","type":"text"},{"code":"Graph","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" law asks.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"strong","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Edges"}]},{"type":"text","text":" are named by the factor edge and the factor vertex that generate them:"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":".first(edge: e, vertexIndex: h)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is G’s edge "},{"code":"e","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" at H’s vertex index "},{"code":"h","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":".second(vertexIndex: g, edge: e)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" is H’s edge "},{"code":"e","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" at G’s vertex index "},{"code":"g","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":". "},{"code":"edges","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" lists every"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"code":".first","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" edge, G-edge outer, then every "},{"code":".second","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" edge, G-vertex outer. When both factors have"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"edge indices, so does the product: "},{"code":"index(e)·|V(H)| + h","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", then "},{"code":"m_G·|V(H)| + g·m_H + index(e)","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Products of multigraphs and pseudographs are defined edge by edge, so "},{"code":"edgeCount","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":", the rows and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"the degrees always agree."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"inlineContent":[{"text":"To materialize it, pass it to a representation’s initializer. Products are ","type":"text"},{"code":"Sendable","type":"codeVoice"},{"text":" when both","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"factors are.","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"The Cartesian product G □ H of two graphs, both directed or both undirected, as a lazy view."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Made by "},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"cartesianProductView(_:_:)"},{"type":"text","text":"."}],"topicSections":[{"title":"Structures","generated":true,"anchor":"Structures","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProductView\/DirectedEdges","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProductView\/EdgeIndexRow","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProductView\/EdgeRow","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProductView\/IndexRow","doc:\/\/GraphProducts\/documentation\/GraphProducts\/CartesianProductView\/UndirectedEdges"]},{"title":"Instance Properties","generated":true,"anchor":"Instance-Properties","identifiers":["doc:\/\/GraphProdu